{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"AC verification pass against the merged tree (worktree detached at 9d5a7278c892, the tip of origin/main; `rebar verify-commit-ticket --rev HEAD` resolves to this ticket).\n\nFIVE of six criteria verified and ticked; AC4 is NOT ticked and the close is NOT run. Detail below.\n\n**AC1 — PASS (ticked).** `pytest tests/unit/store/test_windows_exclusive_leg.py -q` -> `10 passed, 2 skipped in 75.73s` (the 2 skips are the `@_needs_windows` native tests, correct on POSIX).\n\n**AC2 — PASS (ticked).** `-k \"precondition or pid\"` -> `2 passed, 10 deselected`. Substance confirmed by reading the code, not just the test: `_acquire_mkdir`'s docstring (lock.py:334ff) now carries an explicit PER-PLATFORM proof — POSIX `flock` is kernel-mediated on the inode; Windows `msvcrt.locking` byte-range locks live on the kernel file object, enforced machine-wide against every handle and released by the kernel when the owning handle closes, including on abnormal termination. And `lock_owner._signal_zero_probes_liveness()` returns `os.name == \"posix\"`, so `_pid_alive` returns the conservative `True` on Windows and never calls `os.kill` there — reclamation narrows rather than weakens.\n\n**AC3 — PASS (ticked).** `-k no_leg` -> `3 passed, 9 deselected`.\n\n**AC5 — PASS (ticked).** The three named concurrency suites -> `39 passed in 199.70s`. `make lint` clean (including `scripts/check_fcntl_import_guard.py`, the repo's own gate for this defect class). `make typecheck` -> `Success: no issues found in 765 source files`.\n\n**AC6 — PASS (ticked), with a recorded caveat about the AC's own literal check.** The in-session grep returns FOUR matches, not zero: lock.py:8, lock.py:201, lock.py:232, lock_owner.py:355. All four were inspected and are RST double-backtick PROSE inside docstrings (the module docstring's dual-window contract, `_acquire_fcntl`'s and `write_lock_is_busy`'s docstrings, and `lock_owner`'s `fcntl_held` note) — the AC's regex matches its own documentation. The substantive condition holds and is stronger than the AC asks: `grep -rnE '(^|[^_[:alnum:]])fcntl\\.' src/rebar/ | grep -v '``'` returns ZERO across the WHOLE source tree. Every real call routes through the new `_store/lock_kernel.py` selector.\n\nAC6's post-merge clause was also executed (see AC4 below). It reports 115 real `AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'flock'`, not 0 — but every one of them originates in `rebar/reducer/marker.py` lines 33/38/61/67, which this ticket's own Class section declares explicitly OUT of S1's scope (bug `nitro-heavy-bluefish`, the per-ticket archive-marker lock). ZERO come from `lock.py`, `lock_owner.py`, `doctor_locks.py` or `lock_kernel.py`. The store write-lock path — the path AC6 actually names — is clean. Pre-merge baseline for comparison: run 33950878570 (SHA a33333b8, without this fix) had 2593 matching lines, 545 failed / 227 errors.\n\n**AC4 — NOT verified, NOT ticked. This is the blocker.**\n\nAt the time of this pass no merged-main CI run had ever executed the native test: the most recent `sweep (windows)` was run 33950878570 at 2026-09-05T06:49:43Z on SHA a33333b8, which does NOT contain 9d5a7278c892 (merged 08:13:39Z). `sweep (windows)` only fires on `schedule`/`workflow_dispatch`, so the push of the merge did not trigger it.\n\nI therefore dispatched test.yml on main — run **33956014400**, headSha exactly `9d5a7278c892`, job 101279390978, `sweep (windows)` completed `failure` (the job is `continue-on-error: true`; Windows is not yet a declared support target). Full suite: `1518 failed, 16611 passed, 269 skipped, 1375 errors in 5081.80s (1:24:41)`.\n\nThe two native tests do NOT appear anywhere in that log — neither in the FAILED/ERROR list nor by name. Under `pytest -q` a passing test prints no line, so their absence is CONSISTENT with passing (the module collected, and `@_needs_windows` is `skipif(os.name != \"nt\")` so on windows-latest they were not skipped), but it is INFERENCE FROM ABSENCE, not the positive empirical proof AC4 demands. I am not ticking a box on that.\n\n**Discovered work: the story's own test module is 7/12 red on Windows.** Not a production defect — a test-harness portability defect in this story's deliverable:\n\n- 5 ERRORs (`test_windows_leg_write_lock_round_trips`, `..._busy_probe_answers_truthfully`, `..._doctor_probe_answers_truthfully`, `..._excludes_a_second_acquirer`, `..._exclusion_survives_the_mkdir_leg_being_removed`) all fail at test_windows_exclusive_leg.py:169 with `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'fcntl'`. `_windows_shaped_msvcrt()` does `import fcntl as host` to delegate its stub to the host's real `flock`. That is the layer-2 POSIX-HOST SIMULATION; on real Windows there is no `fcntl`, so the fixture cannot build. These tests are meaningless on Windows by construction (the real `msvcrt` is present there) and need a `skipif(os.name == \"nt\")` guard.\n- 2 FAILUREs (`test_no_leg_acquire_refuses_loudly_and_never_takes_the_mkdir_leg`, `test_no_leg_doctor_probe_reports_unknown`) fail with the child reporting `AssertionError: acquire succeeded with NO exclusive leg held`. The child hides only `fcntl`; on Windows `msvcrt` is still importable, so the selector legitimately finds a leg and `acquire()` correctly succeeds. The test's premise — \"NO exclusive leg available\" — is simply false on Windows. This is in fact POSITIVE evidence that the production Windows leg works: the real `acquire()` took the real msvcrt leg on windows-latest.\n\n**Second discovered finding, outside this ticket's file impact:** 1395 of the run's `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'fcntl'` occurrences come from `src/rebar/_store/fsutil.py`, which does a bare unguarded `import fcntl` at :82 and `fcntl.flock` at :92/:98. That is a store module with the same defect class as `marker.py` but I could find no ticket naming it; `lock.py`/`lock_kernel.py`/`lock_owner.py` do not import it, so it is off the write-lock path and does not affect this story's ACs.\n\nNo force override was used anywhere in this pass, and no test was weakened or edited."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788603235001273529,"uuid":"3495837b-3cdc-46f6-9b5c-ceba4d1a4c76"}